Carry the segments execution dropped into the module.

The interpreter records a `data.drop` and an `elem.drop` on the instance, and
nothing of it reaches the external interface, so applying state never wrote a
drop back: a ctor that dropped a passive segment left it whole, and code that
copied out of it after evaluation read what a run would have trapped on. Patch
0001 gave up on any module whose memory could not be flattened if it held a
`data.drop` anywhere, and that took in the start function patches 0008 and
0009 write, which drops each fill's segment: a module this tool had evaluated
could not be evaluated again.

A dropped segment is an empty one. Each application of state now empties every
passive segment the instance has dropped, data and element alike, and the
refusal goes. An active segment was dropped by instantiation and stays, and so
does a fill's segment, whose drop is in the start function this tool writes
and runs again in every instance after the first.

